#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d4/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} ,$ find $\\lambda.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} .\n$$\n\nIn $△ O P C ,$ we have:\n\n- $O P = 2 | \\mathbf{b} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ and $\\mathbf b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O P} = - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- $P C = | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{P C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{P C} = - \\mathbf{a} .\n$$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} \\\\  &  = - 2 \\mathbf{b} + ( - \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\  &  = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderat